WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.416
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.832 --> 00:00:13.000
NARRATOR: December 2018,
astronomers spot strange flares

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.720
coming from a galaxy 250
million light years from Earth--

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.275
GSN 069.

00:00:21.275 --> 00:00:25.260
 We know that GSN 069 has a
supermassive black hole in its

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.390
center equal to
about half a million

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.320
times the mass of the sun.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.240
That's a big black hole.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:37.620
And it blasts out X-rays
in a very, very steady pace

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.300
every nine hours.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.100
Why?

00:00:42.366 --> 00:00:45.100
NARRATOR: The flares are
so energetic and regular,

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.110
the supermassive
black hole must be

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.620
eating the mass of the planet
Mercury three times a day.

00:00:52.081 --> 00:00:54.550
 The big question is, what's
feeding this black hole

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.400
such a huge dinner?

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.160
NARRATOR: In March 2020,
scientists found the answer--

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.090
an unlucky star at
the end of its life

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:08.950
had wandered into the death
zone of the black hole.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.400
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: A
star getting too

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.440
close to a
supermassive black hole

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.380
is like a glazed donut
getting too close to me--

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:18.690
that thing just is
not going to make it.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.080
PHILIP HOPKINS: Stars that
get too close to a black hole

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.580
get torn apart.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.070
They sort of get attacked
by the black hole,

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:26.570
and some of that
material's also getting

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.300
launched off in very
powerful winds, and jets,

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.460
and streams getting out.

00:01:32.935 --> 00:01:35.050
NARRATOR: Somehow
the star survives

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.750
its close encounter with
the supermassive black hole.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.660
Further investigation reveals
it's a small compact star,

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:44.560
a white dwarf.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:50.200
So what makes this tiny
star almost indestructible?

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.630
The answer lies in
how it's formed.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.100
AOMAWA SHIELDS: We
get a clue if we look

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.840
at the life cycle of a star.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.060
It's burning
hydrogen into helium.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.090
That's causing nuclear
fusion, and that

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.700
causes a star to stay stable.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.180
There's this delicate balance
between radiation pressure

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.610
from that nuclear
fusion pushing out

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:13.000
and gravitational
pressure pulling in.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.010
But when stars like our sun
near the end of their life,

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.170
they run out of hydrogen fuel.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.460
NARRATOR: The sun-like star
makes more and more helium,

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.310
which builds up in its center.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.880
Gradually, the immense weight
of the star's outer layers

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.380
crushes the helium core.

00:02:31.188 --> 00:02:33.080
HAKEEM OLUSEYI:
As the core ages,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.750
it gets smaller and
hotter, which increases

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:36.975
the rate of nuclear reaction.

00:02:38.461 --> 00:02:40.570
NARRATOR: These nuclear
fusion reactions

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.450
produce more energy, which
pushes the outer layer,

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:44.620
or envelope, outwards.

00:02:46.186 --> 00:02:47.570
HAKEEM OLUSEYI:
Because there's more

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.730
energy flowing
through the envelope,

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.120
the envelope swells up.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.960
NARRATOR: The star
expands to around

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.715
100 times its original size.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.890
The yellow star
becomes a red giant.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:05.360
Eventually, red giants
shed their outer layers,

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.125
forming stunning gas shells
called planetary nebulas.

00:03:12.326 --> 00:03:16.720
 Planetary nebulae are the
most beautiful objects in space.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.510
They're all spectacular.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.700
AMBER STRAUGHN: A star
that ends its life in one

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.060
of these planetary nebulas
leaves behind a white dwarf

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:24.900
at the center.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.880
And this white
dwarf is essentially

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.562
a cinder, a stellar cinder.

00:03:29.562 --> 00:03:32.600
GIBOR BASRI: It's what's
left after nuclear fusion is

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.375
no longer possible for
that particular star.

00:03:35.375 --> 00:03:36.920
NARRATOR: All that remains--

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:41.240
a glowing white dwarf, the
leftover core of the dead star.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:46.940
But in galaxy GSN 069,
the supermassive black

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.085
hole turbocharged the process.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.160
It stripped off the outer
layers of the red giant

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.725
in a matter of days.

00:03:54.725 --> 00:03:57.240
PHILIP HOPKINS: The black hole
has almost eaten all the juicy

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.880
parts, all the easy-to-get-at
parts of the star,

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.270
leaving behind the sort
of bone, or the leftovers

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.052
of the white dwarf.

00:04:05.052 --> 00:04:06.740
NARRATOR: This
white dwarf is just

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.450
a fifth of the mass of the sun.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.800
How can such a
small star survive

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.286
being so close to a black hole?

00:04:15.286 --> 00:04:18.747
PHIL PLAIT: You might think that
because a white dwarf is small,

00:04:18.747 --> 00:04:20.480
it's not going to last very
long because there's not

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.290
that much stuff there to eat.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:25.586
But it turns out it's
quite the opposite.

00:04:25.586 --> 00:04:29.512
NARRATOR: The pocket-sized white
dwarf is packed full of matter.

00:04:29.512 --> 00:04:31.510
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: If it
were a normal star,

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:33.490
it would have been
shredded long ago.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.390
But because it's such a
dense, tight ball of matter,

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:38.890
it survives.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:43.120
Imagine taking the sun and
crushing it down to just about

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.490
the size of the
Earth, same mass,

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.260
but now packed way more tightly.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.190
So a basketball
worth of this stuff

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.220
would weigh as much
as 35 blue whales.

00:04:55.501 --> 00:04:58.600
NARRATOR: The white dwarf's
extreme density protects it

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.132
from the gravitational onslaught
of the supermassive black hole.

00:05:03.132 --> 00:05:06.070
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: Its orbit
takes it near that black hole

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:07.810
every nine hours.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.870
And every time it encounters
the black hole, some

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:12.975
of its material gets sipped off.

00:05:12.975 --> 00:05:15.490
 They're playing a game
of interstellar tug of war

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:16.930
with one another.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.120
The black hole is bigger,
so it's going to win.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.060
But the white dwarf is very
dense, so it's very tough,

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.797
and it's able to hang in
there for quite a long time.

00:05:24.797 --> 00:05:25.930
HAKEEM OLUSEYI:
It's going to stay

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.060
in orbit around a
supermassive black hole

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.860
for billions of years.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.360
Talk about David and Goliath.

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:35.612
NARRATOR: When astronomers
first discovered white dwarfs,

00:05:35.612 --> 00:05:36.745
they thought they
shouldn't exist.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.230
How could something have
such an extreme density

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:43.240
and not collapse
under its own weight?

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:48.500
Quantum mechanics, the science
of atomic and subatomic

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.360
particles, has the answer.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.660
GIBOR BASRI: We're used
to the rules of physics

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:55.980
up here in the
macroscopic world.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.790
But when you zoom down
into the subatomic world,

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:01.520
things get weird.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:05.720
Here we have the electron,
one of the tiniest particles

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:09.350
in the universe, and it's
these little electrons

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:13.835
that are doing the work of
supporting an entire star.

00:06:13.835 --> 00:06:16.400
 Electrons really
don't like being

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.080
squashed into a small space.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:21.680
If you try to squash too many
of them into too small a space,

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.240
they'll push back really hard.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.970
And this is an effect
called degeneracy pressure.

00:06:27.296 --> 00:06:29.300
NARRATOR: These
degenerate electrons

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.600
stop white dwarfs
from collapsing,

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.656
but they give these
stars strange qualities.

00:06:35.656 --> 00:06:37.860
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: White
dwarfs behave very

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:39.570
differently than normal matter.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.430
Take planets and stars--

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.250
they become bigger
when they gain mass.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.680
White dwarfs are
the exact opposite.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.560
As they gain mass,
they get smaller.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.600
NARRATOR: The more
massive a white dwarf,

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.810
the tighter the electrons
squeeze together

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:56.835
and the smaller and
denser the star gets.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:01.370
The high density means
the white dwarf's

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.740
structure is also strange.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:07.460
It has an extremely thin
atmosphere made of hydrogen,

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.800
or occasionally helium gas.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.320
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: If you were
to take an Earth skyscraper

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.270
and put it on a
white dwarf star,

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.490
if you climb to the
top of that skyscraper,

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:19.070
you'd be outside of the
white dwarf's atmosphere.

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.230
You'd actually be in space.

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:23.570
NARRATOR: Beneath
the thin atmosphere

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:27.170
lies a surface of dense
helium around 30 miles thick.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:32.540
It surrounds an interior made
of superheated liquid carbon

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.880
and oxygen.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:36.920
PHIL PLAIT: A white
dwarf at its surface

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.330
can be a half a million degrees.

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:40.340
It's even hotter
in the interior.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:44.090
And so that kind of material,
it's not going to behave

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:46.230
the way normal matter does.

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:48.920
NARRATOR: Eventually
over billions of years,

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:52.800
the center of the white dwarf
cools down into a solid.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.890
JESSIE CHRISTIANSEN: As
the carbon and oxygen atoms

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:56.930
cool down, they form a crystal.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:59.370
Diamonds are actually
crystals of carbon.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.090
So at the center of these cool
white dwarfs could be a diamond

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.410
the size of the Earth.

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:07.730
NARRATOR: White dwarfs gradually
give off their remaining energy

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:11.090
until there's just a cold,
dead ball of matter--

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:12.425
a black dwarf.

00:08:12.425 --> 00:08:14.990
PHILIP HOPKINS: We'd never seen
what we call a black dwarf,

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:16.910
and there's a simple
reason for that.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.790
It takes a tremendous
amount of time, many tens

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:22.430
of billions of years longer
than the age of the universe

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:23.330
to reach that point.

00:08:24.821 --> 00:08:27.900
NARRATOR: This is the dark
destiny of most mid-sized

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.550
stars, including our sun.

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:35.140
This long, slow death may make
white dwarfs seem ordinary.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:40.150
But these tiny stars could
answer some big questions

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:42.046
about our universe.

00:08:42.046 --> 00:08:45.460
ANDREW PONTZEN: They might be
small, and they might be dim,

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:48.595
but they are essential for
our understanding of physics.

00:08:50.026 --> 00:08:52.310
NARRATOR: New research
into white dwarves

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:55.040
may answer one of the
biggest questions of all--

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.160
can life survive the
death of its star?

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:01.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

